A1. By what can you often tell a wet party? ~ STAGGER
A7. What dry agents do between raids. ~ BOOTLEG
A13. This is what you get when you sum things up. ~ TOTAL
A14. This never made a Frenchman drunk. ~ EAU
A15. This bird is very silly. ~ GOOSE
A16. A preposition. ~ ON
A17. This often makes a kick. ~ BOOT
A19. One of a race of natural born fighters. ~ CELT
A21. As has been stated. ~ SO
A22. What part of the ocean is farthest from the shore? ~ MID
A24. This is sometimes a sign of age. ~ WRINKLE
A26. Possessive pronoun. ~ HER
A27. This is always in a pickle. ~ ACID
A29. This kind of after-dinner speaker is popular. ~ BRIEF
A30. The sound of a popping cork. ~ PING
A31. A kind of bill. ~ NEB
A33. A city with an ancient cathedral and monastery. ~ ELY
A34. Many a New Yorker gets a meal out of this. ~ CAN
A35. This runs around on Al. Smith's brown derby. ~ HATBAND
A38. This is very snappy. ~ ELASTIC
A41. A lot of people get a kick out of this. ~ ALE
A42. This brings up the dirt. ~ ANT
A43. This does not choose to fight. ~ SLACKER
A46. A lot of peaches get this way. ~ SPOILED
A49. Ships do this between ports. ~ PLY
A50. Part of the verb to be. ~ ARE
A52. This is a bird. ~ EMU
A53. On the sheltered side of the ship. ~ ALEE
A55. The age in which men were go-getters. ~ STONE
A57. This caused hard feelings in the first family. ~ ENVY
A59. The highest number on an African golf ball. ~ SIX
A60. This is the house that Jack built. ~ COTTAGE
A62. This wears bell-bottom trousers. ~ GOB
A63. At home. ~ IN
A64. They say this is due for a trip in another world. ~ SOUL
A65. An ancient Roman sport coat. ~ TOGA
A67. A combination that people look up to. ~ WE
A68. What a lot of hope chests are composed of. ~ CEDAR
A70. A prefix meaning out of, outside. ~ EXO
A71. This causes a lot of people to get the "gimmies." ~ GREED
A73. This is always hammering at something. ~ KNOCKER
A74. These are little devils. ~ RASCALS

D1. They say this is on the way to a man's heart. ~ STOMACH
D2. What members of the W. C. T. U. call the liquor they drink. ~ TONIC
D3. Preposition. ~ AT
D4. The Senator from Alabama is noted for this. ~ GAB
D5. What a dab of rouge adds to that school girl complexion. ~ GLOW
D6. What Cal. Coolidge soon will be. ~ RETIRED
D7. This is a chestnut. ~ BUCKEYE
D8. What flappers do when the eyes have "it." ~ OGLE
D9. A little kid. ~ TOT
D10. Get an eye full. (Exclamation.) ~ LO
D11. Where a lot of big guns come from. ~ ESSEN
D12. Pertaining to husbandry. ~ GEORGIC
D18. A sightseer. ~ ORB
D20. This uses Fairy soap. ~ ELF
D23. This is found in most fenders. ~ DINT
D25. This is nothing. ~ NIL
D26. This is very suggestive. ~ HINT
D28. This caused Noah to join the Navy. ~ DEBACLE
D30. Diversion, sport. ~ PASTIME
D32. People who act this way make an ass of themselves. ~ BALKY
D34. This might get somewhere if you paddled it. ~ CANOE
D36. This is very boring. ~ AWL
D37. Brought into the world. ~ NEE
D39. This is never seen in a standing position. ~ LAP
D40. This sets on top of the world. ~ ICE
D43. What some get out of an ocean trip. ~ SEASICK
D44. The point, tip or summit of anything. ~ APEX
D45. What Lizzie was before she became a lady. ~ RATTLER
D46. This is a marble dome in Washington. ~ SENATOR
D47. This is always getting the air. ~ LUNG
D48. These were made for people who stay out all night. ~ DAYBEDS
D51. This is a lot of nonsense. ~ ROT
D54. What men like best—next to themselves. ~ LINEN
D55. A French coin. ~ SOU
D56. Personality. ~ EGO
D58. A single open sound uttered without any constriction of the vocal organs. ~ VOWEL
D60. This stops the flow of liquor in America. ~ CORK
D61. These often get beat-up in the kitchen. ~ EGGS
D64. A kangaroo baby-carriage. ~ SAC
D66. What the rainbow forms. ~ ARC
D69. To cheat or swindle. ~ DO
D72. Each. (Abbr.) ~ EA


